What AI Judge needs:
- Enough detail to open the live app, sign in when needed, navigate to the meaningful screens, and verify deterministic evidence.
- The workflows should prove what the team built, not just that the homepage loads.
- Use the problem, solution, repo, video, demo URL, auth requirement, and contestant notes in APP DETAILS JSON as source material.

Base URL rules:
- Copy the submitted demoUrl from APP DETAILS JSON exactly into baseUrl. Do not infer a different host, substitute the repository URL, or drop a required path prefix.
- Do not move to another origin. The contract baseUrl must share the same origin as the submitted demo URL.
- If the submitted demo URL contains a required path prefix, keep that path in baseUrl or use workflow paths that preserve it.
- Prefer relative paths in steps, such as "/", "/login", "/dashboard", or "settings". Use a full URL only when it is on the same origin.

Authentication rules:
- If the app is public, set auth.required to false and keep all auth credential and selector fields as empty strings.
- If the app requires email/password demo login, set auth.required to true.
- Never include real credentials in the JSON. Set auth.username to "$AUTH_USERNAME" and auth.password to "$AUTH_PASSWORD". AI Judge replaces those placeholders with the stored demo credentials at runtime.
- For email/password login, include loginPath, usernameSelector, passwordSelector, submitSelector, and at least one post-login success check.
- If auth.required is true, workflow 1 must be a complete login workflow whose id, name, or purpose includes the word login.
- Automated judging only supports no-auth or stable email/password demos. Do not use OAuth, SSO, MFA, CAPTCHA, email verification, SMS, or manual approval.
- Never include real secrets, API keys, private keys, production passwords, or personal accounts. Use only throwaway demo credentials supplied for judging.
- If the app requires OAuth, SSO, magic links, passkeys, MFA, CAPTCHA, account pickers, email/SMS verification, paid checkout, or another flow a browser cannot deterministically complete, do not invent selectors. Add specific entries to manualReviewTriggers and explain the blocker in globalRules.notes.

Workflow selection rules:
- Include 2 to 4 workflows for most apps. Use 1 workflow only for very small public demos.
- Put the strongest proof first after login. The first non-login workflow should demonstrate the app's main value proposition.
- Use seeded demo data, stable test accounts, known routes, known labels, and exact expected text whenever possible.
- Cover the important app type without assuming a specific domain: dashboards, CRUD apps, AI chat tools, data visualizations, marketplaces, games, developer tools, mobile-style web apps, internal tools, or content apps.
- Avoid destructive actions unless the app uses disposable test data and the workflow clearly stays within that data.
- Avoid workflows that depend on live third-party services, real payments, real emails, real SMS, private data, or time-sensitive external state.
- Keep each workflow minimal: navigate, perform the key interaction, then assert clear success.

Allowed step actions only:
- goto
- fill
- click
- press
- waitForText
- waitForUrl
- assertVisible
- assertText
- assertUrl

Step object schemas:
- goto: { "action": "goto", "value": "/path-or-full-url" }
- fill: { "action": "fill", "selector": "#input", "value": "text to enter" }
- click: { "action": "click", "selector": "button[type=submit]" }
- click by stable visible text when a selector is not known: { "action": "click", "value": "Start demo" }
- press: { "action": "press", "selector": "#input", "value": "Enter" }
- waitForText: { "action": "waitForText", "value": "Expected text" }
- waitForUrl: { "action": "waitForUrl", "value": "/dashboard" }
- assertVisible: { "action": "assertVisible", "selector": "main" }
- assertVisible by stable visible text when a selector is not known: { "action": "assertVisible", "value": "Dashboard" }
- assertText: { "action": "assertText", "selector": "main", "value": "Expected text" }
- assertUrl: { "action": "assertUrl", "value": "/dashboard" }

Important step-key rule:
- Every action target must use value, not url, text, key, pattern, or requiredUrlPattern.
- requiredUrlPattern is allowed only inside a workflow success block, never inside steps.

Allowed success keys only:
- requiredUrlPattern
- allowedUrlPatterns
- allText
- anyText
- visibleSelectors

Selector rules:
- Use CSS selectors only. Do not use XPath.
- Prefer stable selectors in this order: #id, name, aria-label, placeholder, data-testid, exact href, role-like attributes, then route-scoped CSS.
- Avoid nth-child, generated class names, animation classes, broad selectors, and selectors that depend on viewport-only layout.
- For click and assertVisible, a stable visible text value is acceptable when a reliable CSS selector is not known.

Success check rules:
- Every workflow must include either a success object with at least one meaningful check or final assert steps that prove the desired state.
- Prefer exact visible text, stable headings, stable selectors, and URL patterns that identify the real feature screen.
- For AI/chat workflows, include a known prompt only if the app has a deterministic demo response or seeded behavior. Otherwise assert the UI state that proves the message was submitted and a response container appeared.
- For games or simulations, verify the board/canvas/game area and a deterministic state change such as score, timer, level, or result text.
- For dashboards or data apps, verify at least one meaningful metric, chart/table container, or seeded record.
- For CRUD apps, use throwaway data and verify the created/updated/deleted state without harming real user data.

